client: h1: restart the guys queued on a connection that won't be kept alive

When an h1 response says the connection won't be kept alive (an HTTP/1.0
status line, or a "Connection: close" header, upstream cd4de7456),
lws_client_interpret_server_handshake() takes every request queued on that
connection off its queue to restart them on connections of their own.  On
v5.1.0 that loop moves each of them back to LRS_UNCONNECTED with
lws_wsi_event_role(ww, LWS_WSIEV_RESTART, ...) and sets its user_space to
NULL, but nothing re-drives them: the RESTART event is only a state
transition, their timeout was cleared when they were queued, and their
user can no longer find them from their callbacks.  The requests never go
out and never settle (txiki.js: tests/test-fetch-connection-close-concurrent.js,
"queued fetches never settled").

Keep each dequeued guy's user_space and schedule him, through his
sul_timeout (idle while he was queued), to restart the way a redirect's
restart does in _lws_close_free_wsi_final(): lws_header_table_attach() on
a client in LRS_UNCONNECTED goes on to lws_client_transport_start(), which
makes him a connection of his own (he no longer pipelines).  If that
fails he is closed and his user is told.  Running from the event loop
keeps the restart out of the leader's handshake interpretation and the
vhost lock it holds.  The sul is the sansIO one (struct lws), since
lib/sansio may not touch the IO adjunct's sul_connect_timeout
(LWS_SANSIO_CHECK).

A guy whose close was already decided while he was queued (eg, his user
aborted him with lws_set_timeout(LWS_TO_KILL_ASYNC) in the same service
pass, before the leader's response was read) is not restarted: that kill
is armed on the same sul_timeout, and scheduling the restart there would
replace it, send his request anyway and leave close_decided set, so every
later timeout on him would be ignored.  He is left for the armed kill,
which closes him before he ever connected and tells his user.  A timeout
set on a guy after he was dequeued replaces the pending restart and wins,
as it should (txiki.js: tests/test-fetch-connection-close-abort-queued.js).

v5.1.0 port of txiki.js's lws-h1-restart-queued-on-connection-close.patch
for the v5.0-stable pin ac23cae70.  Not upstream: v5.1.0 (5f39aa483) and
main (d954e68f2) still drop the dequeued guys.  Upstreamable.

---
diff --git a/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c b/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c
index b82fbb680..de91f1199 100644
--- a/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c
+++ b/lib/sansio/http/client/client-http.c
@@ -1538,6 +1538,35 @@ lws_http_client_response_is_interim(struct lws *wsi)
 	return n >= 100 && n < 200 && n != 101;
 }
 
+/*
+ * A guy that was queued on a connection that turned out not to be kept alive
+ * has never had a connection of his own.  Restart him from connect2, where
+ * he will make one.  This runs from the event loop, not from inside the
+ * leader's handshake interpretation that dequeued him, and if the connect
+ * fails, he is closed and his user is told.
+ */
+static void
+lws_client_restart_dequeued(lws_sorted_usec_list_t *sul)
+{
+	struct lws *wsi = lws_container_of(sul, struct lws, sul_timeout);
+
+	/*
+	 * As a redirect's restart does: he is a client in LRS_UNCONNECTED,
+	 * so the attach goes on to start his transport, dns first.  If that
+	 * fails he has been closed, and his user told, and is gone.
+	 */
+	switch (lws_header_table_attach(wsi, 0)) {
+	case LWS_AH_ATTACH_OK:
+	case LWS_AH_ATTACH_WAITING: /* the connect starts when he gets one */
+	case LWS_AH_ATTACH_WSI_GONE:
+		break;
+	default:
+		lws_close_free_wsi(wsi, LWS_CLOSE_STATUS_NOSTATUS,
+				   "restart dequeued: no ah");
+		break;
+	}
+}
+
 /*
  * Returns LWS_HPI_RET_HANDLED, or when the response ended the wsi's part in
  * this pass, what the close of it said: LWS_HPI_RET_WSI_ALREADY_DIED, it was
@@ -1984,7 +2013,21 @@ lws_client_interpret_server_handshake(struct lws *wsi)
 
 				/* go back to "trying to connect" state */
 				lws_wsi_event_role(ww, LWS_WSIEV_RESTART, rops);
-				ww->user_space = NULL;
+				/*
+				 * Keep his user_space: it is his user's, and
+				 * the restart still reports to it
+				 */
+				if (ww->close_decided)
+					/*
+					 * His close (eg, an abort) is already
+					 * armed on his sul_timeout: don't
+					 * restart him, let it close him
+					 */
+					continue;
+
+				lws_sul_schedule(ww->a.context, ww->tsi,
+						 &ww->sul_timeout,
+						 lws_client_restart_dequeued, 1);
 			} lws_end_foreach_dll_safe(d, d1);
 			lws_vhost_unlock(wsi->a.vhost);
 		}
